Sure! To update a break node’s label to `'loopEnd'` only when it isn’t already set to that value, you can use the `updateBreak` function like this:

```javascript
// Replace <nodeString> with the stringified representation of your break node.
const result = updateBreak({
  node: "<nodeString>",   // e.g., '{"type":"Break","label":"oldLabel"}'
  label: "loopEnd"
});
```

If you’re working directly in JavaScript, just pass the actual node object instead of a string:

```javascript
const result = updateBreak({
  node: yourBreakNodeObject,
  label: "loopEnd"
});
```

The function will check the current label; if it differs from `"loopEnd"`, it will create a new break node with that label and return the updated node. Let me know if you’d like help formatting the node string or integrating this into your AST traversal logic!
